infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Generic API design: infers vs forces type args
  desc Same feature, opposite ergonomics — the difference is where inference happens
  compares
    - label Infers (well-designed)
      children
        - label Caller writes values only
          desc emit("login", { userId: 1 }) — autocomplete, zero type args
        - label Type param sits in a parameter
          desc obj: T and key: K give the compiler inference sites
        - label Returns the inferred type
          desc T[K] is precise — string, not the wide constraint
        - label Constraints plus defaults
          desc One signature serves the common and the advanced case
        - label NoInfer pins a second occurrence
          desc TS 5.4 — turns a silent widening into a real error
    - label Forces type args (design failure)
      children
        - label Caller restates the type
          desc Must spell out the type the value already implies — one typo desyncs
        - label No inference site for the param
          desc It appears only in the return, so no value supplies it
        - label Returns the constraint
          desc firstWide([1,2,3]) yields unknown — specificity thrown away
        - label Widening silently accepts bugs
          desc pick(["a","b"], "c") widens C to a union and accepts "c"
        - label Over-clever signatures
          desc Leaked internal types and unreadable walls of compiler errors
theme
  palette #14b8a6 #f43f5e
